Alright we're back so now let's start with Language Takeaway.

26
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:29,930
Language Takeaway.

27
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:37,170
Okay, so in the first part of what Mr. Ford is saying, he mentions something called market share.

28
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:38,950
Now Margot, what is market share?

29
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:46,530
That's right, everyone is competing for market share and this is basically a small portion of your available market.

30
00:02:46,890 --> 00:02:57,350
So for example, if you sell perfumes, women's perfume, then your market share is maybe women who are 18 to 35 years old in your city or in your town or in your country.

31
00:02:57,510 --> 00:02:57,930
I see.

32
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:07,900
Right, so when we're talking about market share because we're sharing a piece of this big pie, we're going to talk about that in terms of a percentage, so 30% or 15%.

33
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:08,460
That's right.

34
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:17,080
I think maybe one of the clearest examples is Coca-Cola has I think maybe a 60% market share of the carbonated beverages market.

35
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:23,660
Interesting, so you could say that Apple has a 50% market share of the personal computing industry.

36
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:24,360
That's right.

37
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:28,980
Very good, so this is what we refer to as market share.

38
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:39,440
Now as we continue to talk about markets, we have two very interesting words when we refer to a developed market and a developing market.

39
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:45,200
Okay so a developed market is something that has been polished and grown over time.

40
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:58,100
Perhaps we talk about the developed world being Western Europe and America, but then we have developing markets and those are markets that are still trying to figure out kind of what's going on.

41
00:03:58,640 --> 00:03:59,020
They're growing.

42
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:02,780
They're usually growing much faster than a developed market.

43
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:10,640
That's right, so again when we're talking about maybe computers, we consider for example the United States a developed market.

44
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:24,600
Many people already have a computer or express the desire to buy one and so maybe a developing market are some of the African countries where they're still growing and so now they're beginning to purchase more and more computers.

45
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:29,680
Okay so developed and developing, two different ways to describe markets.

46
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:29,840
That's right.

47
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:33,360
Now after that we hear this phrase cutthroat.

48
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:37,460
So someone asks why is this market so cutthroat?

49
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:38,100
What does this mean?

50
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:40,820
A cutthroat business or a cutthroat industry.

51
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:43,160
It means that it's very, very competitive.

52
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:50,060
Okay so cutthroat, we're actually talking about maybe having a knife and cutting someone else's throat, but it's just a metaphor.

53
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:53,120
It's a way of saying yes, this is very competitive.

54
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:55,080
So you're not actually cutting someone's throat.

55
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:58,960
Right, everyone is fighting for that market share so it's very cutthroat.

56
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:05,180
Everyone is fighting very, very hard, but you're not literally killing someone or cutting their throats.

57
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:07,920
You could even use this to talk about a sporting event.

58
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:10,620
You could say it was a cutthroat game.

59
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:12,100
That's right, that's right.

60
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:16,520
So anything that's very competitive or very close.

61
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:26,700
So to finish off language takeaway when Mr. Ford was towards the end of his presentation, he said that the rewards could be very, very big in a very short time frame.

62
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,850
So when we say the word time frame, we mean period of time.

63
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:33,320
It's not like 2 p.m.

64
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:37,710
It's like from May to June, we have this short time frame.

65
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:38,970
It's only 2 months long.

66
00:05:39,930 --> 00:05:41,910
So time frame is a window or a period.

67
00:05:42,790 --> 00:05:43,330
That's right.

68
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:51,330
So this is what he is referring to is that in a very short period or in this period of time, they could make a lot of money, a time frame.

69
00:05:51,810 --> 00:05:54,410
But how long would a long time frame be?

70
00:05:54,630 --> 00:05:55,530
Maybe a couple of years.

71
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:58,730
All right, so you can have a short time frame and a long time frame.

72
00:05:59,160 --> 00:05:59,310
That's right.

